{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When I was a young graduate student I got to use \none of the giant telescopes at the Las Campanas  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observatory in the Atacama Desert in Chile. I was \ntraveling with a much more experienced astronomer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from Europe, but it was also his first time in \nthe southern hemisphere observatory. He went  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outside for a weather check and came back looking \nannoyed to report two gigantic clouds in the sky.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I went to look - it was the most \ncrystal clear night I’d ever seen.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Was my colleague suffering altitude \nsickness? Then I realized - there were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two clouds on the sky - two hazy blobs just off \nthe dust-streaked center of the Milky Way band.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I laughed out loud - this wasn’t water vapour \n- this was lunch - the Milky Way’s lunch.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Find a dark night in the southern hemisphere \nand you’ll see too: the several billion stars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the large and small magellanic clouds in \ntheir slow death spiral towards the Milky Way.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "My colleague needn’t have worried - we did some \ngreat observing that night. But astronomers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "watching the resulting collision in around 2 \nbillion years might have more cause for concern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69840,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we scan the heavens with giant \ntelescopes like those on Las Campanas,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see galactic cannibalism everywhere. We see \nmoments that appear frozen on the human timescale,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but are really snapshots of the incredibly \nviolent process of galaxy formation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is how all galaxies are made. We can \npiece together a pretty good understanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this process from countless snapshots. Looking \ninto the distance means looking into the past,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so it’s possible to stitch together a \nfrankenstein flipbook of galaxy evolution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about our home galaxy? It would be nice \nif we could learn the Milky Way’s true history  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and final fate. And, in fact, we can. Since \nthat night on Las Campanas, enormous surveys  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have tracked the positions and motions of more \nthan a billion Milky Way stars. We’re now able  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to calculate a detailed and dynamical map of the Milky Way. We can predict its future mergers with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124080,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Magellanic clouds and Andromeda - but perhaps more astonishing, we can reconstruct its past.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we get to this, let’s review what \nwe know about the Milky Way of the present,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136400,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and of galaxy evolution in the general sense."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Milky Way is a pretty typical barred \nspiral galaxy. Our sun is in the disk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a minor outcropping of one of the spiral \narms. It orbits in the same direction as all the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150320,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disk stars once every 230 Million years. In the \ncenter we have the bulge envelopes - an elongated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spheroid of stars that all orbit randomly at \ndifferent angles. All of this is surrounded by the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "halo - also spheroidal, but twice the diameter of \nthe 100-thousand light year wide disk. This thing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is sprinkled with wayward stars and ancient, \ndense mini-galaxies called globular clusters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But mostly the halo is made of dark matter, \nwhich also suffuses the disk and halo  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183360,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and constitutes 80% of the Milky Way’s mass.The entire galaxy is beautiful and intricately  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190000,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structured. Weird to think that it built itself \ninto this through a life of violence. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s look at what we know about galaxy evolution \nbased on all the other galaxies in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We actually talked about this process recently \nin our episode on the galactic habitable zone.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first galaxies collapsed from very slight \nover-dense regions in the hot hydrogen and helium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas that filled the universe after the Big Bang. \nIt’s hard to see the galaxies in the first billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years or so. Galaxies were still growing, and most \nare too small to see at those great distances.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most distant galaxy known as of the filming \nof this episode was discovered only in April this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year. It shines out from a young universe, only 350 million years after the big bang.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back then, galaxies were raging storms of \nstar formation due to the abundance of gas  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back then. We only see the brightest of \nthose first galaxies. But based on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flipbook that we’ve assembled of the following several billion years we do have a clear picture:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Galaxies assemble from the bottom-up, which \njust means that small clumps form first,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then merge into larger clumps. So \nearly galaxies were messy and mostly small,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and formed stars furiously. These clumps fell \ntogether and spun each other up into whirling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disks, and their violent convulsions settled into \nthe density waves that we see as spiral arms. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new spiral galaxies continued to gobble up \nwispy irregular galaxies to grow that disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s how the Milky Way got to its current \nform. But how can we possibly reconstruct the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "details of such a chaotic process? The stars from \nevery previous merger are mixed all across the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Milky Way disk or through the halo by now. Teasing \nout the Milky Way’s history is sometimes called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stellar archaeology. Perhaps galactic forensics \ncomes is a better description - teasing out the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence of violent encounters from evidence \nthat in some cases has been carefully buried."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s review the evidence. Item 1: Stars that \njoin the Milky Way at the same time, like during  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a merger, should have similar properties. For \nexample, stars that form from the gas of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same galaxy tend to have similar amounts of heavy \nelements in them, as the gas that formed them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was enriched by the same number of supernovae and other explosions. We can measure the heavy element  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "abundance - also called metallicity - by looking \nfor the dips and spikes in a star’s spectrum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that result from specific elements sucking up \nor producing light at specific wavelengths.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347520,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars with similar metallicities could have come"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the same merger events, or were perhaps produced in the same burst of star formation triggered by that event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358080,
    "dDurationMs": 4122,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spectra on their own aren’t really enough to tell if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362202,
    "dDurationMs": 7958,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two stars came from the same galactic snack. So Evidence item number 2: If two stars came from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same merger, they should also have similar \norbits. If their orbital speeds are even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slightly different, they may have drifted \nto opposite sides of the galaxy by now.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are other orbital properties we can \ntry to match. For example, how stretched out, or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eccentric, are their orbits. And the orientation of their orbits relative to the galactic disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see what our forensic investigation has \ntold us so far. A lot, actually. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astronomers have identified the last truly \ngigantic merger that happened to the Milky Way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 10 billion years ago. This merger was so \nlarge - around 50 billion Suns worth of matter -  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it must have reshaped the galaxy and can be \nthought of as the birth of the “modern” Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The galaxy the Milky Way consumed has been \ndubbed ‘Gaia-Enceladus’ - named for the Gaia  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space Telescope which was used to discover the \nevent by identifying star from this devoured  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy in the Milky Way’s halo. The “Enceladus” \npart is for the Greek titan of that name, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has nothing to do with the moon of Saturn. Gaia \nis able to identify the stars from this merger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because of the satellite’s incredible \nability to pinpoint stellar positions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That enables the telescope to detect tiny motions, \nwhich in turn allows astronomers to reconstruct  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453520,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detailed orbits of Milky Way stars. The stars from \nGaia-Enceladus move in highly elongated orbits in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the inner halo of the galaxy, but with a slight \n‘backwards’ bend to the orbit, a clear indication  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that these stars were not born in our galaxy. \nWe’ve also found a group of 13 globular clusters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with matching orbital properties and spectra \nthat were probably once part of Gaia-Enceladus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see more evidence for this past violence in \nthe disk of the Milky Way. That disk has two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parts - there’s the thin disk, which is a few \nhundred lightyears thick, and is the main star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "factory of the Milky way. It’s home to the spiral \narms and the big, bright star forming clouds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495760,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and our sun. We did an episode on why galaxies \nbecome flat disks - but the TLDW is that giant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502880,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas clouds tend to collapse into thin gas disks, and then produce stars that share that geometry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509600,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surrounding the thin disk we have \n- you guessed it - the thick disk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which extends a few thousand light years \nabove and below its more slender counterpart.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not all spiral galaxies have a thick disk, which \nsuggests something special happened to the Milky  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way to create it. It’s made of stars, not gas, \nand these stars orbit just a little faster  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on orbits that are more inclined than the \nthin disk. That causes them to rise above  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and drop below the Milky Way disk, leading \nto the aforementioned thickness. Those stars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are different in other ways - for example, they tend to have fewer heavy elements. That suggests  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they formed before the thin disk stars - around 9 \nbillion years ago, plus or minus a billion years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We already have a perfectly consistent \nexplanation for the origin of the thick disk.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may have been formed during \nthe merger with Gaia-Enceladus.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the stars of Gaia Enceladus got mixed \ninto the halo, the crazy gravitational pulls  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the two galaxies slamming into each other \nkicked up the orbits of many of the stars in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Milky Way’s original thin disk to create \nthe thick disk. Meanwhile, the fresh gas  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576000,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the merger replenished and reformed the thin \ndisk, and triggered a new round of star formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582800,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since its very large and thickening breakfast 10 \nbillion years ago, the Milky Way has only snacked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lightly. But we can trace that history also. \nWhen a dwarf galaxy gets too close to the Milky  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way it gets pulled thin as tidal forces cause \nits near-side to move faster than its rear.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601120,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It gets drawn out into a lengthening tidal \nstream, and ultimately can be wrapped around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxy multiple times. In the end \nit disperses into the Milky Way’s halo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our galaxy has dozens of known streams. Some of \nthe littlest ones are still close together so it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easy for astronomers to pick out the little stripe \nof stars, like GD-1: a globular cluster that’s in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the process of being pulled apart. On the other \nhand, some of the biggest, like the Helmi stream,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contain tens of millions of stars and \nwrap in a full loop around the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the biggest of all the snacks the Milky \nWay has had since the Gaia Enceladus breakfast  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the Sagittarius Dwarf Spheroidal Galaxy \nwhich first fell in about 5 billion years ago.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since then, it’s wrapped all the way around \nthe galaxy, going up and over the poles and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651680,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coming back down to punch through the disk three \ntimes. Most importantly, the massive core is still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relatively intact and moving together, so every \nbillion years or so when it punches through the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Milky Way’s disk its gravity hits like a \nhammer. Actually, more like a drum stick."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the mass of the galaxy approaches, it pulls \nthe disk of the Milky Way up, but then when it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passes through it pulls it back down. Like \nbeating a drum or plucking a guitar string,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the stars oscillate up and down in the galactic \nplane, making a very faint ripple through the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disk. As best we can tell, the three passes \nthat the core of the Sagittarius Dwarf have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made through the disk correspond to three \nepisodes of star formation in the Milky Way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and one of these passes even happens to line up \nwith the formation of our sun and solar system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "4.5 billion years ago. Now, we’re not saying the \nSagittarius Dwarf galaxy is fully responsible for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711280,
    "dDurationMs": 6192,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the existence of our solar system, but we're not saying it's not.  It’s certainly highly plausible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717472,
    "dDurationMs": 4368,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All together, we know of up \nto 7 mergers in the history of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxy and at least 42 distinct \nstreams surrounding the Milky Way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and as we continue to study the data from \nthe Gaia mission we’re likely to find more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This, finally, brings us to the present, as the \nMilky Way prepares to have its lunch and second biggest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meal yet. The Milky Way’s two brightest satellite \ngalaxies, the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are currently making their first pass. Already, \nwe see them being pulled apart- the Magellanic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Clouds have massive tails that make a great \nloop all across the ‘bottom’ half of the galaxy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a 600,000 lightyear long tail of \ngas called the ‘Magellanic Stream.’"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while the Magellanic clouds themselves \nare only about 1% the mass of the Milky Way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves, the entire stream may be 10 billion \nsolar masses due to the enormous amount of dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter it contains, making this easily the biggest meal the Milky Way has seen since  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gaia Enceladus. When that merger happens the Milky Way will get a fresh infusion of gas, probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "triggering another bout of star formation in about \n2 billion years. The accompanying supernova waves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may not be the best thing for life on Earth, but \nwe do have 2 billion years to get ready for that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of the Milky Way’s past mergers have been \n‘minor’, meaning that the Milky Way was always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "significantly more massive than its meal. That’s \ngoing to change with the final merger of our local  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808960,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "group of galaxies - when Andromeda and the \nMilky Way collide. This is a major merger,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because Andromeda is a full-blown spiral galaxy \nin its own right. In fact it’s around twice as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive as the Milky Way. We’ve gone into the gory \ndetail of this collision in a previous episode.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Go ahead and watch that one if you want to see \nour galaxy get a taste of its own medicine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834000,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for now let’s enjoy these short \nfew billion years of being the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838080,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biggest and hungriest kid in the playground,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840880,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as we gobble up any galaxies foolish enough to stray into the Milky Way’s little patch of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848160,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we get to \ncomments, I wanted to let you know about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Search.pbsspacetime.com. This incredible tool \nallows you to search the entire Space Time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "catalog for any word or phrase and get links \nto the time code where I mention those words.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s an incredible resource, built entirely \nby one of our fans: Vegard Nossum. Vegard,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we hear at Space Time can’t thank you enough for \nthe creativity and ingenuity of your work. There’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a link in the description, so everyone can start \nexploring Search.pbsspacetime.com right away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878160,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re doing comment responses \nfor the last two episodes:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the one on the galactic habitable zone and its implications for the fermi paradox, and the one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where we showed why space is not expanding inside gravitationally bound systems like the Milky Way.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's start with the habitable zone:\nIn that episode I said that parts of the galaxy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with too much heavy element abundance might not \nproduce life because those systems would produce  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too many gas giants. Some of you asked why that’s \nthe case. It’s simply because gas giants form when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a large enough rocky or icy core forms to start \nholding on to hydrogen and helium atmospheres.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915360,
    "dDurationMs": 7108,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That needs to happen before the star turns on and blasts away all of the lighter gases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922468,
    "dDurationMs": 5772,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few of you pointed out that even a system with \nlots of gas giants could have habitable moons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that’s true. There’s a good reason to restrict ourselves to Earth-like systems when we do these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculations of the abundance of life-bearing \nworlds. It’s because we’re trying to find the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conservative number for possible origins of life \nto help explain the Fermi paradox. We want to find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out if even the most conservative estimate still \ngives us a large number, because that tells us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we need to do work to look to other things to explain the absence of evidence of technological life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956720,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of you pointed out other \npossible explanations for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959280,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fermi paradox and the specialness of Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lucas Nicholson and others remind us that \nthe Earth’s moon is exceptionally large,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and such large moons are probably very \nrare even if Earth-mass planets are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "common. The large moon has been proposed as \nan important factor in the appearance of life,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which may have first appeared in tidal \npools. The tidal pools hypothesis  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no longer the clear frontrunner for abiogenesis. \nFor example, geothermal vents on the ocean floor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987600,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are looking good option for the origin of life, and they don’t care about the moon. These are also found on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas giant moons - so that boosts our \npotential abiogenesis location number quite a bit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Thatcher told us about another idea that I hadn’t heard of - that the late  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heavy bombardment may have been needed \nto seed the surface of the planet with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high abundance of heavy elements - which was \nperhaps necessary for life to get started.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The late heavy bombardment was a massive \nmeteor shower that lasted millions of years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and probably isn’t something experienced by \nmost terrestrial planets. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some scientists doubt that the late heavy \nbombardment even happened - all of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence is from cratering on the moon, \nand biases in the analysis of that data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033680,
    "dDurationMs": 7353,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may have led to miscalculations of the bombardment rate. OK, on to the expanding universe stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041033,
    "dDurationMs": 6247,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "SnufkinMatt asks “If space doesn't expand \ninside a gravitational field, then what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens at the boundary between this and where \nspace is expanding? Would the expanding space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "try to 'drag' neighbouring space with it and would \nyou get a kind of tug-of-war between the two?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes and no. First, assuming no cosmological \nconstant and dark energy, the expansion of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe does not continue to tug on \nthe space within gravitationally bound  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions. However those bound regions fell \ntogether from matter that was initially  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving apart in the expanding universe, and \nthat kinetic energy affects that final form  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the bound system — so I would think thank \ngalaxies that formed in an expanding universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086960,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be more puffed up than those \nthat formed in a static universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090880,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nathaniel Cleland responded to that comment to address the \ndark energy issue. He rightly points out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the Schwarzschild metric isn’t really valid in a universe with a cosmological constant.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104000,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There would indeed be an additional effect \ndue to the tiny vacuum energy inside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxy. However the effect is minuscule, and \ncouldn’t really be characterized as a tug of war.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would result in a very very slightly larger size to the Milky Way than without dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121120,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eric Marsh asks if space inside \ngravitationally bound systems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1125040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually contracts, rather than simply not \nexpanding with the rest of the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130800,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is kind of, yes. You can interpret the \nmath that way. There is a valid interpretation of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity in which we can say that \nspace is flowing inwards in a gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1142560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. You may have heard me say that space \nflows across the event horizon of a black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the speed of light - that’s the velocity \nof an inertial frame that falls from infinite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153280,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance towards a black hole. In the same way, an \ninertial frame at the cosmological event horizon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160240,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is flowing away from us at the speed of light. Evelyn always tells people not to imagine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1166800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intergalactic space as expanding, but rather \nthat galaxies are shrinking. Jonathan Rose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a similar hypothesis that all matter is \nconstantly shrinking within a static space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Listen, guys, I understand that as smart, \nscience-educated people it’s extremely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tempting to make up almost-plausible \nnonsense to fool the “common folk”.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1189760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Believe me, I feel the temptation as powerfully \nas anyone. But neurological studies have shown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1196240,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that people who accept OR \npropagate science misinformation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1200160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are 50% more likely to suffer degenerative \nmicrocephaly in later years. In other words,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1206720,
    "dDurationMs": 7157,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everyone’s brain shrinks like those galaxies. \nI think I can feel it happening right now."
    } ]
  } ]
}
